## 198. Universal Classification Formula

Let:

```text
S=\{x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_n\}
```

Find a property \(P\) such that:

```text
P(x_1)=P(x_2)=\cdots=P(x_{n-1})
```

but:

```text
P(x_n)\neq P(x_1)
```

Then:

```text
\boxed{x_n=\text{odd one out}}
```

The index need not be \(n\); whichever item fails is the answer.

---

## 199. Final Master Checklist

Before answering, ask:

```text
□ What domain are the items from?
□ What do most items have in common?
□ Is the relationship semantic or mathematical?
□ Is the property objective?
□ Does the rule fit every non-outlier?
□ Does exactly one item fail?
□ Is there a simpler rule?
□ Am I confusing surface similarity with actual category?
□ If pairs are given, did I compare the relationships?
□ If figures are given, did I check rotation/reflection?
```

---

